# {{PLUGIN_NAME}} — {{ORG_NAME}}'s agent harness, as an opencode configuration

This directory *is* the configuration. It ships the same harness {{ORG_NAME}} runs
elsewhere — the verbs, the agent contracts, the gates — packaged for **opencode**.

> **Owned by {{ORG_NAME}}.** It is generated, but it is yours: identity, version and
> distribution are the org's, and nothing here is a runtime dependency on a generator.

## Install

**Globally** (every project on this machine):

```
cp -R ./ ~/.config/opencode/
```

**Or per repo** (checked in, so a team shares one harness):

```
mkdir -p <repo>/.opencode && cp -R ./ <repo>/.opencode/
```

Either way the layout is the same:

```
opencode.json    model + the one-provider allowlist + the subagent allowlist
AGENTS.md        session preamble (points at the wiki; run /{{VERB_PRIME}} first)
agent/           {{BUILD_AGENT}}.md — the build graph's worker (capped by `steps`, cannot dispatch/spawn/ask); validate.md — the OPTIONAL read-only supplementary reviewer (present only when enabled)
rubric/          review · {{VERB_GATE}} — the checklists a worker applies to its own diff (the launcher must manage this dir)
node/            the graph nodes a worker reads by path, not preloaded: every non-entry node + each main-thread graph's index (the launcher must manage this dir)
command/         {{VERB_INTRO}} · {{VERB_SETUP}} · {{VERB_PRIME}} · {{VERB_INCEPTION}} · {{VERB_REFINE}} · {{VERB_EXECUTE}} · {{VERB_WIKI}} · {{VERB_HANDOFF}}
skill/           the procedure each command reads and follows, plus each worker graph's index + entry node skill
plugin/          dispatch.js — the thin worker launcher (below); reminders.js, wiki-pull.js — advisory nudges
```

**Restart opencode after copying.** Config, agents, commands and plugins are read at
startup — an already-running session will not see them.

## Upgrading

Re-run the generator over your existing org config and re-emit; the package is rebased
onto the newer generator. The intra-task graph is declared once in the shared top-level
`graph:` block of the org config (the states one build agent traverses, its loop caps,
and the optional supplementary reviewer) — both the opencode and Claude Code targets bind
the same graph.

## Authenticate

No credentials live here. Log the provider in once with opencode itself:

```
opencode auth login      # pick {{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}
```

If `{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}` needs provider options (a region, a profile, a base URL), add
them under `provider.{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}.options` in your own `opencode.json` — that is an
opencode setting, not part of this harness. Default model: `{{OC_DEFAULT_MODEL_REF}}`.

## One provider

The load-bearing control is the **allowlist**: `opencode.json` sets

```
"enabled_providers": ["{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}"]
```

so `/models` lists **only** `{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}` — including on a machine that already
has an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or `OPENAI_API_KEY` in its environment, which opencode would
otherwise auto-detect as extra providers. `"disabled_providers": ["opencode"]` is kept
as belt-and-suspenders (it names the built-in Zen provider explicitly), but a deny-list
alone does **not** cover a provider you did not think to name — the allowlist does. The
`dispatch` tool enforces the same provider and the org's banned list per task.

## The graphs: one worker agent per worker graph

The org config declares a catalog of graphs (ADR 0019). Each **worker** graph emits its
own agent file — the build graph's is `agent/{{BUILD_AGENT}}.md` — so the orchestrator
(your session, `{{OC_PRIMARY_AGENT}}`) and a worker never share a definition. The worker
walks understand → build → validate → **review (self-check)** → fix → clear in one
context; the relied-upon independent review is the **human merge gate + CI**, never a
second agent inside the loop. It is capped by `steps`, denies `dispatch`, `subagent` /
`task` and `question`, and runs at the org floor (the session's configured model) unless
a dispatch sets `model`.

- **Workers → `dispatch`** — one call launches one declared worker for one task.
  `dispatch` launches **only** the workers the catalog declares (its `WORKERS` table),
  each with its isolation: a per-(repo, ticket) git worktree, or in place for an
  isolation-none worker. A worker is a **root session**, so it shows in the native session
  list — the fleet view — with no custom UI (ADR 0017).
- **Fan out** several workers only for genuinely-independent tasks (a valid DAG of
  repo × concern). A single coupled task is never split across agents.
- **One provider.** `enabled_providers` is an **allowlist**, so only `{{OC_PROVIDER_ID}}`
  is offered even on a machine with an ambient `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` / `OPENAI_API_KEY`;
  `disabled_providers` is kept alongside it (a deny-list only covers providers someone
  thought to name).

**The optional supplementary reviewer** (`agent/validate.md`, present only when enabled)
is the ONE surviving fresh-context validator: for a high-risk diff, a read-only pass over
a **completed** PR through the native subagent tool (`subagent_type: "validate"`). Its
permissions derive from its own frontmatter (`{{OC_VALIDATE_DENY_LIST}}` denied, `bash`
an allowlist of the tracker's and git's read commands; a denied permission is removed from
the toolset *and* refused at execution), and its model is pinned at definition time. It
reviews the finished artifact and never interleaves inside the build loop.

**The spawnable set is allowlisted.** `opencode.json`'s `task` permission rule denies `*`
and allows `{{OC_PRIMARY_AGENT}}` (the orchestrator) — plus `validate` when the
supplementary reviewer is enabled. No worker is task-spawnable: a native child runs in the
parent's location, never its own worktree. A typo'd `subagent_type` is denied, never silently the
full-permission primary agent, and a hand-added agent file is not spawnable until the org
config names it.

## Host compatibility

One artifact, two hosts: this configuration runs **unchanged on opencode 1.18.29+ and
on opencode 2.x**. `plugin/dispatch.js`, `plugin/reminders.js` and `plugin/wiki-pull.js`
carry both entrypoints — 2.x loads `setup()`, 1.18.29+ loads `server()` — and both
halves drive the same model policy and worktree invariant. The rest of the layout
(`agent/`, `command/`, `skill/`, `opencode.json`) is understood by both hosts; 2.x
translates the 1.x `permission` block automatically (`task` → `subagent`, `bash` →
`shell`). Host-specific mechanics are mapped, not duplicated — e.g. the worker's
completion postback is `session.prompt({resume:false})` on 2.x and
`session.promptAsync({noReply:true})` on 1.x.

Known differences on 2.x:

- `instructions` entries in `opencode.json` are accepted but not loaded by 2.x — it
  reads `AGENTS.md` instead, which this package ships with the same wiki pointers.
- The session-start toast nudge is 1.x-only for now: 2.x has no server-side toast
  API. The compaction handoff line (the load-bearing half) works on both hosts.

## `dispatch` — the thin worker launcher

The orchestrator (your session) fans out one **writer worker** per call and gets its
id back at once: `dispatch({ agent, ticket, repo?, model?, variant?, task_id?, command? })`.

- **A worker is a root session at a worktree.** `dispatch` creates it at a git worktree
  of `repo` (one per (repo, ticket)) running the declared worker you name (`agent`). The
  ticket is the whole envelope — the worker reads it from the tracker, so you never
  re-author a prompt. Because workers are **roots** (no `parentID`), the **native session
  list is your fleet view** — this plugin ships no UI (ADR 0017).
- **Non-blocking + poll-free.** `dispatch` returns the `task_id` immediately. A detached
  waiter blocks to the worker's idle, reads its result, and posts a `<run-closed>` line
  back with `resume:false` — admitted without waking a turn, so it surfaces on your **next
  turn**. You never block and never spin an autonomous loop.
- **Fan out and loop.** Several calls in one turn run in parallel; `task_id` continues the
  same worker (the feedback loop — e.g. re-dispatch after a FAIL with the deficiencies as
  `command`); `model` (optionally `variant`, which needs an explicit `model` and a 2.x
  host) picks the model inside the org policy (one provider, banned list). Unset `model` =
  the org floor; the launcher pins no default of its own.
- **The result.** The `<run-closed>` line carries the worker's RESULT line (the last
  `RESULT:` line it wrote), so the orchestrator reads a verdict, not a transcript.

Each builder traverses the whole build → review → fix loop
itself — review is a self-check node, not a separate dispatch. The only fresh-context
validator, the optional supplementary reviewer, runs through the native subagent tool
(`subagent_type: "validate"`) on a **completed** PR, never through `dispatch`; `dispatch`
owns only the writer's worktree grant.

Workers are remembered in `.worktrees/.dispatch.json`, so a restart keeps `task_id`
continuation working. `dispatch` fails closed on a missing agent, a call with neither a
ticket nor a `task_id`, an escaping repo, a banned/off-provider model, or a foreign
`task_id`. When a ticket is done, remove its tree:
`git -C <repo> worktree remove <workspace>/.worktrees/<repo>--<ticket>`.

## `plugin/reminders.js` — advisory, never blocking

Two nudges, both fail-open: a toast when a top-level session starts (run
`/{{VERB_PRIME}}`), and one line pushed into the compaction summary so a cycled session
knows the durable state lives in the tracker and `{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}`. Child run sessions
are not nudged.

## `plugin/wiki-pull.js` — advisory, never blocking

On session start it fast-forwards the local org brain (`${{ORG_WIKI_PATH_ENV}}`, falling
back to `{{ORG_WIKI_DEFAULT_PATH}}`) to `origin/main` — but **only** when that clone is on
`main` and has a clean working tree, so `/{{VERB_PRIME}}` reads current decisions. It runs
`git fetch` + `merge --ff-only`; it never merges a divergent branch, never touches a dirty
tree, and every step fails open — a fetch failure or an offline machine can never break a
session.

